## The user's look wins

What the generator shipped is a starting point, not a constraint. When the
user asks for another look — the other shipped template, a brand, "make it
look like X" — **do it**. Do not keep the current look because this file
describes it, and do not answer that the project has a design system.

{% if is_web %}
This service renders its pages on the server, so its look is
`static/app.css` and the templates beside it: restyle those. For a Vue or
React frontend with one of the shipped looks, generate one:

```bash
jfast new service admin --kind spa --frontend vue --template nexora    # or classic
```
{% else %}
This project was generated with the **{{ frontend_template }}** look. The
choice is recorded in `.jfast-template` (`frontend_template`), and
`jfast new view` reads it from there so new pages match.

| The user wants | Do this |
| --- | --- |
| The other shipped look (`nexora` or `classic`) in a new project | `jfast new service <name> --kind spa --frontend {{ frontend }} --template <look>` |
| The other shipped look here | Generate it beside this one with the command above, then move the `src/Modulo<Name>/` folders across. Pages draw from the shared components, so they follow |
| A look jfast does not ship | Restyle here: replace the tokens in the stylesheet below, then the markup of the components in `src/components/`. Keep their props — generated pages call them |

After a restyle, pass `--template` to `jfast new view` if the recorded look no
longer describes the project, or edit the page it writes.
{% endif %}

**"Use a token, never a raw colour" is about consistency inside whichever look
the user chose.** It is not a reason to keep the default. A restyle changes
the tokens; components keep reading them.

{% if is_web %}
## Tokens

`static/app.css` holds the tokens, as CSS custom properties on `:root`. Add one
there before using it. Every token is defined on bare `:root` and only
*redefined* in the dark block, so no colour has its single definition inside a
media query.

| Token | Use it for |
| --- | --- |
| `--accent` / `--accent-text` | The one action on the screen. `.btn-primary` is this pair |
| `--bg` | Page background |
| `--surface` | Cards, inputs, secondary buttons |
| `--border` | Dividers and outlines |
| `--text` | Body copy |
| `--muted` | Labels, hints, timestamps, empty-state copy |
| `--danger` | Destructive actions and error text |
| `--ok-bg` / `--ok-text`, `--off-bg` / `--off-text` | The two badge states |

One family, from the token `--font` — the system UI stack. Spacing is
`var(--space)` and multiples of it; radius is `var(--radius)`, except badges
and avatars, which are pills.
{% elif frontend_template == "nexora" %}
## Nexora: tokens

Liquid glass: frosted panels over a black studio (a light one too), a glass
sidebar, an island top bar, a WebGL ribbon behind everything.

`src/nexora/nexora.css` is the design system. Colour comes from two places:

| Tokens | Hold |
| --- | --- |
| `--c-accent`, `--c-glow`, `--c-body`, `--c-accent-deep`, `--c-on-accent` | The palette, as RGB triplets: `rgb(var(--c-accent) / 0.2)` |
| `--bg-primary`, `--card-bg`, `--card-border`, `--pill-bg`, `--nav-bg` | Surfaces, per theme |
| `--text-main`, `--text-dim`, `--text-light`, `--accent-text` | Text, per theme. `--accent-text` is the accent made readable |
| `--success`, `--warning`, `--danger`, `--info` | Status. The hue is the meaning, so these never follow the accent |

`src/style.css` exposes them as Tailwind utilities for layout work:
`bg-surface`, `bg-panel`, `border-line`, `text-ink`, `text-ink-soft`, and the
`brand-50`…`brand-900` ramp, derived from the accent.

**The accent is runtime state.** `src/nexora/accent.js` derives the whole
palette from one colour and writes it on `<html>`: from the viewer's pick in
`AccentPicker`, else `VITE_ACCENT` in `.env`, else the triplets in the
stylesheet. To rebrand, set `VITE_ACCENT` or edit those triplets — never a
colour in a component. The brand name is `VITE_APP_NAME`, read through
`src/nexora/brand.js`.

## Nexora: components and classes

Use the components first: `BaseButton`, `CardBox`, `BaseInput`, `BaseModal`,
`BaseBadge`, `EmptyState`, `SkeletonLoader`, `SectionTitleLineWithButton`.
When markup needs a class directly, these exist:

| Class | For |
| --- | --- |
| `card-panel`, `liquid-panel` | A pane of glass; the second has no padding |
| `btn-modern` + `btn-primary` / `btn-outline` / `btn-ghost` / `btn-danger` | Buttons, pill-shaped |
| `badge-status` + `is-success` / `is-warning` / `is-danger` / `is-info` / `is-accent` | Status, always with a word |
| `page-header-row`, `page-title`, `nx-eyebrow`, `panel-head`, `panel-title` | Page and panel headings |
| `kpi-grid`, `kpi-card`, `kpi-label`, `kpi-value` | Figures |
| `table-responsive`, `erp-table`, `cell-title`, `cell-sub` | Tables |
| `nx-list`, `nx-list-item` | A column of small items |

Type: Plus Jakarta Sans for text, JetBrains Mono for figures and labels, Caveat
for the wordmark only. The ribbon (`src/nexora/liquid.js`) is decoration:
never put information in it, and never import it outside `background.js`.
{% else %}
## Classic: tokens

`src/style.css` holds the tokens, in Tailwind v4's `@theme`. Add one there
before using it, and use the utility Tailwind generates from it — `bg-surface`,
`text-ink`, `border-line` — rather than a literal value.

| Token | Use it for |
| --- | --- |
| `brand-600` / `brand-500` | Primary action, hover, focus ring. The full 50–900 ramp is declared |
| `surface` | Page background |
| `panel` | Cards and raised surfaces |
| `elevated` | A surface on top of a panel — menus, hovered rows |
| `line` | Dividers and outlines |
| `ink` | Body copy |
| `ink-soft` | Labels, hints, timestamps |
| `ink-faint` | Placeholder and disabled text |

The surface tokens are declared in `@theme inline`: the utilities emit
`var(--ui-surface)` instead of a value resolved at build time, so swapping the
variable flips the whole interface. That is why no generated component carries
a `dark:` class. One family — the system UI stack; spacing steps
`2 / 3 / 4 / 6 / 8`; `rounded-lg` for cards and inputs, `rounded-md` for
buttons, `rounded-full` for badges.
{% endif %}

## Rules that hold in any look

- **The accent is an accent, not the palette.** It marks the one thing on a
  screen that should be clicked; three of them is no accent at all.
- **A destructive button is never solid.** Solid is already "proceed".
- **Emphasis is weight and size, never colour.** Two type sizes on a screen is
  usually right.
- **Borders over shadows on dark surfaces.** A shadow on near-black is
  invisible.
- **Loading is a skeleton shaped like what is coming; empty says what would be
  there and offers the action that creates it.**
- **Never `outline: none` without a replacement.** Keyboard users lose the
  page.
- **Colour is never the only signal.** A red badge also says "Failed" in words.
{% if not is_web %}
- **No `dark:` class in a component.** The tokens already change with the
  theme; a component that hardcodes both halves is two themes waiting to
  diverge.
{% endif %}

## Verification

- Every screen in all three theme states: system, `data-theme="dark"`,
  `data-theme="light"`.
- Every colour is a token of the look in use.
- The focus ring shows on keyboard navigation; icon-only buttons have an
  `aria-label`.
- At 375px wide nothing scrolls the page sideways.
{% if not is_web %}
- `npm run build` passes, and a page from `jfast new view` still renders.
{% endif %}
